From: Sam Day <me@samcday.com>
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] driver core: honor an explicit probe timeout without modules

An RPM power-domain provider may appear asynchronously after initcalls
finish, even when every driver is built in. With MODULES disabled,
driver_deferred_probe_check_state() currently returns -ENODEV immediately
after initcalls_done, ignoring an explicit deferred_probe_timeout.
Consumers such as SDHCI then fail permanently before the provider arrives.

Keep returning -EPROBE_DEFER while a positive requested timeout remains
pending. Nonpositive values retain the existing built-in-only behavior,
including the default zero: return -ENODEV after initcalls. A positive
timeout uses the existing timeout work and returns -ENODEV after expiry.
Module-enabled behavior is unchanged.

This only changes the missing-provider result. It does not postpone the
MODULES=n fw_devlink_drivers_done()/fw_devlink_probing_done() calls or
make the wider sync_state policy equivalent to a module-enabled kernel.

Base: pem120/linux-msm89x7 a51b91b503307d35902447dd1f90db765372cf3b
Assisted-by: LLM

diff --git a/drivers/base/dd.c b/drivers/base/dd.c
--- a/drivers/base/dd.c
+++ b/drivers/base/dd.c
@@ -278,7 +278,8 @@
  * @dev: device to check
  *
  * Return:
- * * -ENODEV if initcalls have completed and modules are disabled.
+ * * -ENODEV if initcalls have completed, modules are disabled and no
+ *   positive deferred probe timeout remains.
  * * -ETIMEDOUT if the deferred probe timeout was set and has expired
  *   and modules are enabled.
  * * -EPROBE_DEFER in other cases.
@@ -288,7 +289,8 @@
  */
 int driver_deferred_probe_check_state(struct device *dev)
 {
-	if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MODULES) && initcalls_done) {
+	if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MODULES) && initcalls_done &&
+	    driver_deferred_probe_timeout <= 0) {
 		dev_warn(dev, "ignoring dependency for device, assuming no driver\n");
 		return -ENODEV;
 	}
